    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, our offensive engine is centered on two high-upside speed threats who draw elite matchups against favorable opposing profiles. **Corbin Carroll** (81.55) is our strongest play, benefiting from a high-efficiency matchup against Pallante, where both the park factors and a slow opposing delivery elevate his stolen base probability to an elite tier. Similarly, **James Wood** (76.28) carries an elite xwOBA profile into a matchup with Lopez, whose 5.64 xERA suggests a prime opportunity for extra-base hits. Both players are bolstered by favorable batting order placements, ensuring they maximize their plate appearances against sub-optimal pitching.\n\nThe decision to bench **Kyle Karros** (41.78) was our tightest call of the day; while he draws a boost from a favorable park environment, his efficiency is stifled by a difficult matchup against Greene. Ultimately, we prioritized the platoon advantages and higher scoring floors of **Travis Bazzana** and **Austin Riley**, despite their own challenging matchups, to keep our daily ceiling intact. We are keeping a close watch on **Travis Bazzana**, as facing Skenes represents a significant hurdle that drags his score down to 42.09, sitting right at our marginal threshold. While **Gabriel Moreno**, **Josh Naylor**, **Brice Turang**, **Cole Young**, **Isaac Paredes**, **Mickey Moniak**, **Seiya Suzuki**, and **Tyler Soderstrom** all clear our starting requirements, we must remain vigilant for any late lineup shifts that could erode the projected value of our lower-scoring starters. Outside of our active roster, **Trevor Story** and **Giancarlo Stanton** remain sidelined, while we continue to monitor the utility of bench depth pieces like **Jose Caballero**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Max Muncy**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Alex Freeland**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Henry Bolte** for future utility.",
